{"step_index":474,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-30T18:34:03Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-31T00:04:03+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-31T00:04:03+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/Downloads/priyanshu%20readme/youtube/Stickman/implementation_plan.md`\nTotal Lines: 245\nTotal Bytes: 10157\nShowing lines 1 to 245\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # Execution Plan v3 — Updated Aug 18, 2026\n2: \n3: > All previous versions had wrong assumptions. This one aligns with your actual corrections.\n4: \n5: ---\n6: \n7: ## What Changed From v2\n8: \n9: | v2 (Wrong) | v3 (Fixed) |\n10: |---|---|\n11: | Both channels from Day 1 | **Stickman scripts FIRST**, Nishchay study parallel, Nishchay production later |\n12: | Whop = find clients, cold DMs | Whop = community marketplace. Join, pick up RPM deals, clip, upload. |\n13: | Filled in niche details | **No niche plans given yet.** Plan waits for your input. |\n14: | 30-day monetization math | Removed. Don't bring up unless asked. |\n15: | Voice cloning recommendation | Deleted. |\n16: | 20-step daily plans | ADHD-friendly: next 1-2 actions only. |\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## The Sequence (Your Decision — Don't Rearrange)\n21: \n22: ```\n23: PHASE 1 (NOW):     Stickman → ideas, competitor study, scripts\n24: PHASE 1b (PARALLEL): Nishchay → READ competitor scripts (study only, no production)\n25: PHASE 2 (AFTER):   Nishchay → production begins once study phase improves your scripting\n26: ONGOING:           Clipping (Whop + Vyro) → see clipping/ folder for details\n27: ```\n28: \n29: **Split: 60% Stickman / 40% Nishchay**\n30: \n31: ---\n32: \n33: ## Production Pipeline (Per Video — End to End)\n34: \n35: ### Step-by-Step Workflow\n36: \n37: ```\n38: Step 1: TOPIC VALIDATION\n39:     → Check project_brief.md in youtube/Stickman/projects/[NN]_[name]/\n40:     → vidIQ keyword validation (check competition score)\n41:     → vidIQ title scoring (aim for 85+, front-load emotion words)\n42:     → Confirm topic from phase1 deep research rankings\n43: \n44: Step 2: RESEARCH (Day 1)\n45:     → Read phase1_part1 or part2 for topic's deep research\n46:     → Watch 2-3 competitor reference videos\n47:     → Compile facts and sources into research_notes.md\n48: \n49: Step 3: SCRIPT (Day 1-2)\n50:     → Use /prompt-forge to write a tight scripting prompt for the AI\n51:     → Write hook (first 30 seconds — pattern interrupt)\n52:     → Full draft (~1,800 words for 12 min)\n53:     → While writing: activate /humanizer + /ai-writing-gaps + /stop-slop\n54:       (these run DURING writing, not as separate passes)\n55:     → /humanizer: kills surface AI tells (vocabulary, em dashes, rule-of-three)\n56:     → /ai-writing-gaps: fixes structural problems (pacing, sources, contrast)\n57:     → /stop-slop: removes predictable AI patterns from prose\n58:     → Save as script_v1.md in project folder\n59: \n60: Step 4: VOICEOVER (Day 2)\n61:     → ElevenLabs generation with emotional tags\n62:     → Save voiceover_notes.md\n63: \n64: Step 5: VISUALS (Day 2-3)\n65:     → Nano Banana 2 image generation per scene\n66:     → Google Omni video clips where needed\n67:     → Visual plan saved as visual_plan.md\n68: \n69: Step 6: ASSEMBLY (Day 3)\n70:     → Remotion/FFmpeg composition\n71:     → Captions added\n72:     → 4K export (local or GitHub Actions)\n73: \n74: Step 7: POLISH & PUBLISH (Day 3)\n75:     → Thumbnail designed (3 concepts, pick best)\n76:     → Title + description + tags finalized (vidIQ scored)\n77:     → vidIQ tag optimization (use volume-proven tags, avoid 0-vol keywords)\n78:     → vidIQ description template with timestamps\n79:     → Upload to YouTube\n80:     → Update project_brief.md checklist\n81: ```\n82: \n83: ## Updated Video Priority Order (Phase 1 Deep Research — Aug 17, 2026)\n84: \n85: | Priority | Topic | Composite Score | Project Folder |\n86: |----------|-------|:-:|----------------|\n87: | **Video 1** | The Most Terrifying Weapons Ever Banned | 94 (title: 93) | `projects/01_banned_weapons/` |\n88: | **Video 2** | Every Banned Book in History Explained | 83 | `projects/02_banned_books/` |\n89: | **Video 3** | Dumb War Tactics That Actually Worked | 74 | `projects/03_dumb_war_tactics/` |\n90: | **Video 4** | Every Monster from Greek Mythology Explained | 89 | `projects/04_greek_mythology_monsters/` |\n91: | **Video 5** | Every Type of Bullet Explained | 91 | `projects/05_bullet_types/` |\n92: \n93: > [!NOTE]\n94: > Full deep-dive research for each topic: `niche_research/phase1_part1_topics_1_to_10.md` and `phase1_part2_topics_11_to_20.md`\n95: > Project briefs with production checklists: `projects/[NN]_[name]/project_brief.md`\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## Tool Stack\n100: \n101: ### Research & Scripting\n102: | Tool | Use |\n103: |------|-----|\n104: | NotebookLM | Upload competitor videos, extract insights, gym audio |\n105: | Gemini (web app) | Native video + audio analysis |\n106: | Antigravity IDE | Script writing, research, commands |\n107: | vidIQ (Web App) | Script Writer, Title Generator, Daily Ideas, Learn ONLY |\n108: | vidIQ MCP | Keyword research, outliers, channel stats (5 free accounts) |\n109: \n110: ### Voice & Music\n111: | Tool | Use |\n112: |------|-----|\n113: | ElevenLabs | AI voiceover with emotional tags |\n114: | Google Lyria 3 | Background music matched to scene mood |\n115: \n116: ### Visuals & Assembly\n117: | Tool | Use |\n118: |------|-----|\n119: | Nano Banana 2 | AI image generation |\n120: | Google Omni | AI video clips (2-10 sec) |\n121: | FFmpeg | Local video assembly, audio mixing, 4K export |\n122: | Remotion | React-based programmatic video editing + animated captions |\n123: | GitHub Actions | Free cloud rendering (2,000 min/month) |\n124: \n125: ### Polish\n126: | Tool | Use |\n127: |------|-----|\n128: | CapCut | Captions + color grading |\n129: | Agent Reach | Web scraping for live data |\n130: \n131: ### Script Quality Skills\n132: | Skill | When |\n133: |-------|------|\n134: | `/prompt-forge` | Before writing — creates a rigid, optimized prompt for the AI |\n135: | `/humanizer` | During writing — kills 33 surface AI tells |\n136: | `/ai-writing-gaps` | During writing — fixes 17 structural problems |\n137: | `/stop-slop` | During writing — removes predictable AI prose patterns |\n138: \n139: ### Tool Rules\n140: - Do NOT call vidIQ MCP without permission\n141: - Always mention credit costs before suggesting vidIQ operations\n142: - vidIQ web app: Script Writer, Title Generator, Learn, Daily Ideas ONLY\n143: - Full tool details + setup: `_toolkit/toolkit.md`\n144: \n145: ## vidIQ Integration (What vidIQ Does Better)\n146: \n147: | Pipeline Step | Use vidIQ For | Use Antigravity For |\n148: |---------------|---------------|--------------------|\n149: | Topic validation | Keyword volume + competition scores | Deep topic research + rabbit holes |\n150: | Title | Title scoring (0-100), aim for 85+ | Hook architecture + paradox structure |\n151: | Thumbnail | Scoring + niche benchmarks | Concept design + image generation |\n152: | Tags | Character-counted tag lists with volume data | — |\n153: | Description | Template with timestamps + hashtags | — |\n154: | Post-publish | Retention curves, drop-off analysis, traffic sources | Strategic interpretation of the data |\n155: | Competitor analysis | Live engagement %, VPH, breakout ratios | Forensic analysis of why things work |\n156: \n157: ### Key vidIQ Findings (Aug 19, 2026)\n158: - \"banned weapons explained\" = 0 search vol. Use CTR titles, search-optimized tags.\n159: - \"dark history explained\" = best comp-to-volume ratio (29.1 comp, 13.8K vol)\n160: - \"stickman animation\" = 1.5M monthly volume (huge discovery potential)\n161: - Serious History (819K) = direct stickman competitor, 3.9% engagement\n162: - **⚠️ Feb 1, 2027: Monetization threshold doubles (4K → 8K watch hours)**\n163: - Full vidIQ data: `niche_research/vidiq_data_aug19.md`\n164: \n165: ## Multi-Agent Mode (When Ready)\n166: \n167: Using `/teamwork-preview` in Antigravity, run parallel agents:\n168: - Agent 1: Analyze top 10 channels in your niche via vidIQ\n169: - Agent 2: Research trending keywords\n170: - Agent 3: Build a content calendar\n171: - Agent 4: Clipping research (see clipping/ folder)\n172: \n173: All running simultaneously. Use this AFTER your first video is published, not before.\n174: \n175: ## Daily Schedule Blocks\n176: \n177: | Block | Time | Hours | Use |\n178: |---|---|---|---|\n179: | 🏠 Home Work | ~9-11 AM | 1-2h | Light tasks, planning, prep for library |\n180: | 🚶 Travel | ~11-12 PM | — | To library |\n181: | 📚 Library (Deep Work) | 12-6:45 PM | ~7h | Main work block — laptop + unlimited 5G. Scripting, Remotion, research, production |\n182: | 🏋️ Gym (Passive) | 7-10 PM | 3h | NotebookLM audio + voice notes to WhatsApp \"Capture\" group between sets |\n183: | 🟢 Light Work | 10 PM-12 AM | 1-2h | Progress check, leftover tasks, 3 bullets in WhatsApp \"Daily Log\" at 11:50 PM |\n184: | 🛏️ Sleep | 12 AM | — | Hard cutoff — phone away |\n185: \n186: Total: ~9-10.5h active + 3h passive. 6 working days/week. Thursday = college + REST (library closed).\n187: \n188: > **Current 7-day checklist**: See `7day_checklist_aug11-17.md` for Notion-ready daily tasks.\n189: \n190: ---\n191: \n192: ## Phase 1: Stickman — Production Mode\n193: \n194: **Niche research = COMPLETE.** Deep research for all 20 topics done. Video order decided.\n195: \n196: **Current task:** Produce Video #1 (Every Banned Weapon in History Explained)\n197: 1. Open `projects/01_banned_weapons/project_brief.md`\n198: 2. Follow the production checklist\n199: 3. Use `/prompt-forge` to write the scripting prompt\n200: 4. Deep research already done: `niche_research/phase1_part1_topics_1_to_10.md` → Topic 1\n201: \n202: ---\n203: \n204: ## Phase 1b (Parallel): Nishchay — Study Mode\n205: \n206: **Goal**: Absorb competitor scripting patterns before producing anything.\n207: \n208: **What needs to happen:**\n209: 1. Build a Nishchay competitor database (5-10 channels doing IAS/IPS/DM dramatic content)\n210: 2. Read/analyze their top-performing scripts — dramatic structure, hooks, tension curves\n211: 3. Note what patterns repeat across winning videos\n212: \n213: **This is study time, not production time.** You're sharpening your scripting before you produce.\n214: \n215: **Tools for this:**\n216: - `vidiq_video_transcript` (5 credits) — pull competitor scripts directly\n217: - `vidiq_outliers` (5 credits) — find which videos are outperforming their channel average\n218: - NotebookLM — feed competitor transcripts, generate audio summaries for gym\n219: - Gemini web app — paste video URLs, ask for dramatic structure analysis\n220: \n221: ---\n222: \n223: ## Clipping (Moved to clipping/ folder)\n224: \n225: All clipping details (Whop + Vyro) now live in `clipping/` folder. See:\n226: - `clipping/clipping_overview.md` — overview and TODO\n227: - `clipping/whop/whop_guide.md` — Whop marketplace details\n228: - `clipping/vyro/campaigns.md` — Vyro campaigns and rates\n229: \n230: ---\n231: \n232: ## ADHD Rules (Non-Negotiable)\n233: \n234: 1. **Production before consumption.** No YouTube, no tutorials, no research until the day's production block is done.\n235: 2. **One thing at a time.** Don't start video #2 until video #1 script is done.\n236: 3. **Phone in another room** during deep work.\n237: 4. **If you've been \"planning\" for more than 30 minutes, you're procrastinating.** Start writing.\n238: 5. **No new OneTab saves.** If you find a video, write the title on paper. Review weekly.\n239: \n240: ---\n241: \n242: ## Your Next Action\n243: \n244: **Open `projects/01_banned_weapons/project_brief.md` and start the production checklist.**\n245: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":475,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-30T18:34:03Z","thinking":"**Analyzing Workflow Integration**\n\nI've been examining the `implementation_plan.md` for the \"Stickman\" YouTube channel, focusing on its highly structured, ADHD-friendly workflow. My current analysis centers on how best to integrate this workflow with the GTA Kids research. Specifically, I'm thinking about the Phase separation (Study and Production) and the strict 7-Step Pipeline outlined.\n\n\n**Adapting The Pipeline**\n\nOkay, so I realized GTA 5 Kids videos are voiceless, scriptless. The \"Stickman\" pipeline needs adjusting. Step 1 (Topic Validation) remains valid, using vidIQ. Step 2 (Research) now focuses on competitor video analysis via XML. Step 3 (Script) becomes Scene & Macro Planning, ditching `/humanizer`. Visuals will use Nano Banana and Omni, and Assembly likely via Remotion/FFmpeg/CapCut.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"ArtifactMetadata":{"RequestFeedback":false,"Summary":"A comprehensive summary of the 11 sub-agents' deep research into the GTA 5 Kids niche, covering the production loopholes, hardware reality, monetization hacks, and algorithm expectations.","UserFacing":true},"CodeContent":"# GTA 5 Spider-Man Kids Niche: Deep Research Summary\n\nThis document synthesizes the findings of the 11 specialized sub-agents dispatched to investigate the realities of starting a GTA 5 superhero channel in 2026.\n\n## 1. The Production \"Speedrun\" Loophole (1-2 Hours/Video)\nOpus 4.6 assumed videos took 5.5-7.8 hours. The sub-agents found that overseas content farms use extreme automation to bypass this:\n*   **XML Templates:** They don't build sets. They download pre-made `.xml` files of massive ramps and obstacle courses via the **Menyoo Object Spooner** (from gta5-mods.com).\n*   **Auto-Combat & Puppet Macros:** They use **JulioNIB's Patreon scripts** for auto-combat, and the **Scene Director** mod to bind NPC animations to Numpad keys (e.g., Press 1 to make Venom dance).\n*   **The Compilation Hack:** They only record **3 to 5 minutes of new gameplay**. The remaining 15 minutes is old videos stitched to the end to hit the 20-minute mark for mid-roll ads.\n*   **The Render Skip:** Instead of exporting a massive file from the Rockstar Editor, they hide the HUD (press H) and screen-record the playback with **OBS** to save hours of render time.\n\n## 2. The Monetization Hack (\"Reused Content\" Bypass)\nWhy did MARINARA ($74K/mo) succeed while Nemo ($0/mo) failed? \n*   **Nemo:** Posted raw, unedited 3rd-person gameplay. YouTube's AI flagged this as unoriginal/reused.\n*   **MARINARA:** Treated the game as a Machinima animation studio.\n*   **The Formula:** To bypass the filter without a human voice, you must use the **Rockstar Editor Free Cam** to create custom cinematic angles. Mute the game's original audio completely. Overlay royalty-free music and spam cartoon sound effects (boings, whooshes) to prove human editorial effort. Add text or speech bubbles to create a \"narrative.\"\n\n## 3. The Real Risks (FTC COPPA and Disney Myths)\n*   **COPPA Fines:** The FTC has *never* fined an individual YouTuber. The $170M fine was against Google. The real risk is YouTube forcing the \"Made for Kids\" label, dropping ad rates by 80%.\n*   **Disney Strikes:** Highly unlikely. Disney doesn't strike modders. The only risk is **Take-Two** (Rockstar's parent) if you try to monetize leaked assets.\n*   **The Real Danger (Elsagate):** If you make Spider-Man do something violent, sexual, or mature in GTA 5, YouTube will ban the channel. Keep it 100% slapstick PG.\n\n## 4. Laptop Hardware Reality ($800 Minimum)\nRunning GTA 5 + Heavy Mods + OBS recording causes severe thermal throttling on laptops.\n*   **The Minimum Setup:** Must have an **NVIDIA GPU** (RTX 4060 minimum) to use the NVENC encoder in OBS (saves CPU). You absolutely need **32GB RAM** (16GB will crash custom maps). \n*   **Recommended Laptops:** Lenovo Legion Pro 5i or ASUS ROG Strix (thick chassis with excellent cooling).\n*   **Mandatory Mod Fixes:** You must install **HeapAdjuster**, **Packfile Limit Adjuster**, and a **Custom Gameconfig.xml**. If you don't, the game will crash (`ERR_MEM_EMBEDDEDALLOC_ALLOC`) the moment you spawn Spider-Man.\n\n## 5. Thumbnail Psychology\n*   **Formula:** 200% saturation. David vs. Goliath setup (normal hero vs. 50-foot giant villain). Exaggerated facial expressions. Neon red arrows/circles. Almost zero text.\n*   **Workflow:** Freeze time in Menyoo -> Scale up villain -> Rockstar Editor Free Cam -> Zoom in -> Turn on Depth of Field (blur background) -> Take screenshot -> Open in Photoshop -> Crank vibrance to +100 -> Add white outer glow stroke to hero.\n\n## 6. Day 1 Algorithm Expectations\n*   **0 Views is Normal:** The algorithm tests new channels on tiny batches. Expect 5-30 views per video in the first month.\n*   **The Shorts Bridge:** 100% long-form is too slow for discovery. Make 10-second Shorts of the craziest stunts and use the **\"Related Video\" link** feature to funnel viewers to the 20-minute video.\n*   **First Video Time:** Even with shortcuts, the first video will take **10 to 20 hours** due to the steep learning curve of fixing game crashes, installing OpenIV, and mastering Menyoo hotkeys.\n\n## Conclusion\nThe $74K/month success is a high-volume arbitrage. It relies on 95% free assets (gta5-mods.com), a strict 20-minute compilation hack, and a $30 Cloud PC or $1,000 laptop. The true barrier to entry is the initial frustration of mod instability.","Description":"Compilation of the 11 sub-agents' deep research into the GTA 5 Kids Niche realities.","Overwrite":false,"TargetFile":"C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\efc75235-355e-455c-b598-1a3e644f6697\\gta_kids_subagent_research.md","toolAction":"Creating research summary file","toolSummary":"Save subagent research"}}]}
